Original commit body from 441246f213a47441a4b11bcf9d678049e7fa4c4e:

auto-pr printed "🧹 Cleaning up local branch..." and then deleted
neither the local branch nor anything else. Not intermittent — a 100%
failure on the default path, invisible because both commands were
written `2>/dev/null || true`.

Root cause: auto-pr merges via REBASE (scripts/lib/github-api.sh tries
methods=(rebase merge), rebase first). A rebase replays the PR's commits
onto the base under new SHAs, so the local branch tip is never an
ancestor of the updated base — and that ancestry test is exactly what
`git branch -d` applies. Reproduced:

    $ git branch -d jgstern-agent/docs/spec-ir-nullability-and-dst-ref
    error: the branch '...' is not fully merged.        exit=1

This is the same trap already recorded in the repo's own notes — `git
branch --merged` lies where auto-pr rebases — and `-d` uses that test.

The `git push --delete` beside it was dead code.
_github_delete_pr_head_branch already removes the PR head branch via the
API at merge time, so the push could only fail with "remote ref does not
exist" (also reproduced). Meanwhile nothing pruned the stale
remote-tracking ref it left: `git pull` does not prune and fetch.prune
is unset, so one accumulated per merge. Seven branches piled up in a
single day; an earlier session found 97 local branches and 436
remote-tracking refs from this.

Fix: check with `git cherry` (patch-ids, so a rebased-and-merged commit
registers as upstream) and then `git branch -D`; keep the branch with a
warning if it carries any commit not upstream, rather than forcing;
replace the dead push with `git fetch --prune`; stop swallowing errors.

The script already knew: the vPR-flush path uses `git branch -D`, and
the manual-recovery hint it prints tells the user to run `git branch -D`.
Only the post-merge path used `-d`.

Guard: tests/test_autopr_branch_cleanup.py drives the real cleanup_local
against a real bare origin. Getting the harness honest took three
corrections, each of which had made it pass against the broken code:
`push -u` gave the branch an upstream (`-d` also accepts
merged-into-upstream, and auto-pr never sets one — it pushes a bare
refspec); the precondition assertion compared a stale tracking ref; and
cherry-picking onto an unchanged parent reproduces a byte-identical
commit, so it modelled a fast-forward rather than a rebase. The harness
now asserts its own preconditions so it cannot quietly stop testing the
defect, and a third case pins the property `-d` existed to protect: a
branch holding a commit that never landed upstream is kept, not deleted.

